Camoufox: pages see the chrome:// files and about: pages of the claimed OS.

A page can load every file of a contentaccessible chrome package and can tell
a registered chrome package, resource host or about: module from an absent one
(new Worker() returns for the first and throws for the second). A Linux build
claiming Windows served Linux's set: aboutWebauthn.* and about:webauthn, no
aboutThirdParty.*, aboutWindowsMessages.*, printPageSetup.*, migration brand
PNGs, default-agent image, gfxsanity, about:third-party or
about:windows-messages, and the Linux preprocessing of browser.xhtml,
places.xhtml, license.html, buildconfig.html and others (cfparity cross-os
static.json and page/namespaces, run 6b: 29 files, 5 about: pages, 2 names).

- ManifestParser: a `camouos=<os>` modifier registers a line only for an
  identity claiming that OS (MaskConfig::GetSpoofedOS, the build's own OS when
  none). `os=` still matches the build's OS, so the browser UI is unchanged.
  A `camoucontent` modifier on an override puts it in a second table.
- nsChromeRegistry: ConvertChromeURLForLoad consults that table first for a
  load a page triggers (a web, null, expanded or moz-extension principal;
  query and ref ignored), from nsChromeProtocolHandler::NewChannel. The
  browser's own loads (system, about:, chrome:, resource:) keep the build's
  files. The table reaches content processes in OverrideMapping.contentOnly.
- toolkit/camoufox-overlay (browser/additions, generated per Firefox version
  by scripts/gen-os-overlay.py from the stock Windows and Linux releases)
  holds the stock bytes and the manifest lines; mozpack learns both flags.
- about:third-party, about:windows-messages and about:webauthn are compiled
  into every desktop build, and nsComponentManagerImpl::Init invalidates the
  ones a stock build of the claimed OS lacks, in every process, so a lookup
  fails NS_ERROR_FACTORY_NOT_REGISTERED as for a module never built.
  about:crashes and about:studies are registered always, as stock does on
  every desktop OS (Camoufox builds without the crash reporter and Normandy).

Caches: chrome:// stylesheets are cached by URL alone (Loader.cpp KeyEquals),
so gen-os-overlay.py refuses a content-only override of a subresource the UI
could also load; markup only runs as a document, which a page cannot load from
chrome://, and the XUL prototype cache holds system-principal documents only.

Not covered: aboutDialog.xhtml's MOZ_UPDATER deck (a build flag, not OS-keyed);
a macOS claim keeps the build's files (no stock macOS set is recorded).

Guards: browser/tests/playwright/patches/contentaccessible-parity.py (one
stock record per claimed OS) and chrome-overlay-claimed-os.py.

